            if (New_Ret)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount);
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount);

                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            }
            else
            {
                // We use the fact that New_arrListIndexOfX is -1 when x is not present in the ArrayList
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This test method covers what is necessary for a Pex to understand that `Contains()` method does not change the state of an array list.

## PexSettings
PexSettings provides a way to control Pex from the code. Each PexSettings property can be used to alter the behavior of Pex on a test method or class.

Here are a few commonly used settings:

- **MaxBugs:** Defines the maximum number of bugs Pex will report.
- **MaxStates:** Defines the maximum number of states Pex will explore. 
- **Mutator:** Specifies the mutator to use when exploring the states.
- **RandomSeed:** Controls the randomization process used by Pex. Setting the same seed will allow Pex to produce the same results during multiple runs of the suite.
- **Verifier:** Controls the verifier that Pex will use. The verifier determines how Pex verifies its results.

```csharp
[PexClass(MaxBugs = 5, MaxStates = 100, Mutator = Mutator.Covariant, 
    RandomSeed = 42, Verifier = typeof(PexExplorationVerifier))]
public partial class YourTestClass
{
    // your test methods here
}
```

In the above example, Pex will try to find up to 5 bugs, explore up to 100 states, use the Covariant mutator, use a seed of 42 for the randomization, and use the PexExplorationVerifier to verify the results.

Keep in mind that using `PexSettings` can have serious consequences on your tests, as they can dramatically influence how Pex behaves. For example, setting `RandomSeed` to the same value will cause Pex to produce the same results each time it is run.

## PexInline
`PexInline` can be used to simplify tests by allowing you to quickly exercise private methods or properties under test. It allows you to pass the method name as a string to the `PexInline` method, and Pex then wraps the call in a method call.

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void MyTest([PexInline(TargetMethod = "MyClass.MyMethod")] Action action)
{
    action();
}
```

In this test method, Pex will generate the `MyMethod`, wrap it in an `Action`, and execute it. All edge cases are explored by `[PexMethod]`, which Pex will use to form the `MyClass.MyMethod`.

The method supplied to `PexInline` does not need to be testable in itself and it doesn't need to be public.

## PexObserve
`PexObserve` can be used to observe the value of an object or the return value of a method. This information is useful when Pex is exploring the states of your system and may be useful for debugging and understanding the tests. 

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void MyTest(...)
{
    int result = MyMethod(x);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("MyMethod result", result);
}
```

In this example, `result` of `MyMethod(x)` will be observed and its value will be displayed in the Pex Visual Studio plugin when you run your tests.

## Summary
In this post we have explored the fundamentals of Pex testing. I hope this gives you a good starting point for using Pex to test .NET applications. There is a lot more to learn and discover about Pex as you delve into its documentation and tutorials. Happy testing!!
