## Mocking

Mocking is a way of programming which allows you to replace parts of your system under test with fake implementations, in order to test the way your system behaves in isolation.

In .NET, popular mocking frameworks include Moq, Rhino Mocks and JustMock. 

For example, using Moq:

Install-Package Moq

```csharp
//Arrange
var mock = new Mock<IMyService>();
mock.Setup(foo => foo.GetValue(It.IsAny<string>())).Returns(10);
var service = mock.Object;
```

It's important to keep in mind that mocking objects should be used sparingly. This is because mocking introduces an extra level of complexity into your system and can reduce the apparent readability and maintainability of your code. In general, mocking is most useful in testing the interaction between different components in your system. It should not be primarily used to mock out specific functionality that you believe is brittle or poorly--------------------
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == (Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0)); 
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount); 
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX); 
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == ArrayList.Contains(x));

	}
    }
}
The test was successful at the beginning. When I run the test by calling the Contains method, I get an exception stating something like: "Could not invoke the method because it is being used as a test and does not exist."
Should I rename the method or what could be the reason for this problem?

A: 
What could be the reason for this problem?

The problem seems to be related to how Pex and Moles are using a proxy class for the ArrayList type. When they call methods on the ArrayList instance, they are calling those method on the proxy/stub class. If those methods have a custom implementation in the ArrayList class, it might not work as expected.

Should I rename the method or ...?

Although not recommended because it may prevent automated testing, one workaround would be to rename your Contains method to a name that is not likely to conflict with other method names. 

Pex uses IL weaving to create test stubs for your classes. When it tries to test the Contains method, it tries to call the Contains method of the weaved stub rather than calling the actual implementation in the ArrayList class. It is this stub that does not have the original Contains implementation but rather has a stubbed Contains implementation to return true.
Even if you are not using any Moles interactions in your Pex tests, you will still get those Moles interactions since Pex uses them to create the test stubs.

If you would like to work around this issue, try one of these options:

1. Reorganize your project so the tests don't reference the target assembly.
2. Use Moles in your test code to replace the Contains method with your own implementation.
3. Use a tool like Moles Reflector to generate the stubs that contain the original methods rather than just the stubs. However, this could create other problems since Moles Reflector only works for .NET 4 clients and not for Silverlight or .NET 4.5.
4. Use a source code transform tool (like CSMT) to modify your code just before running the tests to replace the Contains method with a custom implementation. This would require manual work and would also prevent you from using the Moles feature, so you would have to manually create the stub. You could do something like this:

```csharp
// Original
[TestMethod]
public void TestMethod1()
{
    var list = new ArrayList();
    ArrayList.Contains = (x) => true;
    ...
}

// Transformation
[TestMethod]
public void TestMethod1()
{
    var list = new ArrayList();
    list.Contains = (x) => true;
    ...
}
```

Note: The last option is quite nasty and can lead to maintenance problems in the future, especially if these changes are needed in different tests. You can use it if Moles and IL weaving just can't be used for these reasons.
5. The best solution is to refactor your code so the tests don't need to deal with the concrete type. You might make an interface and have the ArrayList implement that instead:

```csharp
public interface IList
{
    bool Contains(object value);
}

public class ArrayList : IList
{
    public bool Contains(object value)
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```

Now, your tests can only deal with the interface and not with the concrete class. With this approach, you can replace the actual ArrayList with a stub or a mock during your tests without having to modify your production code.
6. In most of the cases, the best thing to do is to move away from ArrayList and use .NET collections interfaces like IList, ICollection, IEnumerable. Since .NET collections are more versatile and support more functionalities than traditional ArrayList.

As you can see, there are many different solutions and each solution has a trade-off. The best solution really depends on your specific needs and constraints.

Please let me know if you have further questions.


A: I would suggest using a mocking framework to simulate the behavior of the ArrayList class--------------------	    //	 @@@ If the return value is true, the method IndexOf() returns a value that is less than or equal to the method LastIndexOf(),  
	    //	 @@@ And the Count property before is equal to the Count property after the call to method Add(Int32)

            // If the return value is false, index of x before is less than or equal to after
            if (!New_Ret)
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount - 1);

            // If the return value is true, the method IndexOf() returns a value that is less than or equal to the method LastIndexOf()
            if (New_Ret)
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            // And the Count property before is equal to the Count property after the call to method Add(Int32)
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}
```

## Conclusión
Como puedes ver, Microsoft Fakes es un conjunto de herramientas que simplifica la creación de mocks para las pruebas unitarias de código administrado, alineado con los principios y prácticas de las bibliotecas de .NET. Como se puede ver en el código del ejemplo, se ha utilizado Microsoft Fakes para crear un entorno controlado que permite aumentar la confiabilidad en las cosas internas de la clase que se va a probar. 

Estas son solo algunas de las ventajas de usar Microsoft Fakes para las pruebas unitarias, ya que brinda una manera sencilla de emular los comportamientos de las bibliotecas externas durante las pruebas, lo que aumenta la confianza en su código.

Este es solo el principio; también existen casos prácticos donde el uso de Microsoft Fakes puede reducir considerablemente el tiempo necesario para las pruebas unitarias y, en casos más avanzados, pueden mejorar el rendimiento de las pruebas al proporcionar un entorno controlado para las operaciones costosas o bloqueadas. Porque el verdadero poder de las pruebas unitarias está en su capacidad de proporcionar un entorno aislado y controlado donde las acciones de prueba pueden interactuar con su entorno natural sin que esto afecte a la integridad de la prueba.

Por supuesto, la inclusión de pruebas unitarias no debe reemplazar a las pruebas funcionales y de integración. Sin embargo, las pruebas unitarias con Microsoft Fakes brindan una excelente cobertura por sobre el límite principal del código administrado, donde es difícil (o imposible) de determinar qué está ocurriendo bajo el capó.

El hecho es que, aunque Microsoft Fakes puede parecer un poco intimidante para las personas nuevas en las pruebas unitarias, al integrarse con otras herramientas como el generador de código estático de prueba unitaria (con la CLR Test) y el análisis de código, las ventajas resultantes del equipo de desarrollo se vuelven ajustadas a medida que las pruebas se vuelven más complejas

Fuentes: 

- [Microsoft Fakes - Página principal](https://github.com/Microsoft/VisualStudioOnlineDataServices/wiki)
- [Introducción a la pruebas unitarias con ejemplo usando Microsoft Fakes](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dn263171.aspx)
- [Pruebas unitarias con Microsoft Fakes y Isolator](https://www.codeproject.com/Articles/109658/Unit-Testing-using-Microsoft-Fakes-and-Isolator)
