```--------------------            if (!New_Ret) {
		// This is a manual fix, the IndexOf method return -1 if an item is not found in the list.
		// We consider that the position decrement is due to the removal of an element.
		// Thus, the index should not decrease.
		Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX.Equals(Old_arrListIndexOfX) ||
			      New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
		Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX.Equals(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX) ||
			      New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
		// If the removed index is not found, it will have decreased by 1,
		// otherwise, the index should not have changed
		Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX.Equals(Old_arrListIndexOfX - 1) ||
			      New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX);
		Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX.Equals(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX - 1) ||
			      New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            }
            else {
		// If the item is found, the index must not have decreased
		Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
		Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Please note that the actual verification code may need to adjust based on the specific implementation and behavior of the ArrayList class.

### Relevant assertions in the contract test
The following assertions have been made to ensure the correctness of the ArrayList.Contains method:

1. If the return value is false, the index of x before the removal should be less than or equal to after the removal (i.e., index should not decrease).
2. If the item is found, the index must not have decreased.

These assertions help to verify the correctness of the ArrayList.Contains method in terms of maintaining the integrity of the indexes after the removal. 

## Limits
This framework was designed to work with the .NET framework, and has limitations. Some of these are:

- The framework uses the PexObserve and PexAssert methods to provide additional information about the execution context, which may not work in all testing frameworks.
- The framework's ability to test specific methods from .NET's framework classes is somewhat limited. It is specifically designed to test methods used in the contract testing example.
- The coverage of the framework is not exhaustive, and may not cover all situations or corner cases.
- The framework assumes that the .NET framework is used for testing, which may not be the case with other programming languages or different versions of the framework.

Keep in mind that while contract testing can be a powerful tool, it should not be the only form of testing used. It is always recommended to use a combination of different types of testing, such as unit tests, integration tests, and end-to-end tests, to ensure a comprehensive test suite.


